<wpt lat="37.36672" lon="-88.44268"><name>2-fer US-3791 + US-3823</name><cmt>Guide pin: On the Northern Route (Trail of Tears) inside the park</cmt><desc>US-3791 Trail of Tears National Historic Trail + US-3823 Livingston County Wildlife Management Area. On the Northern Route (Trail of Tears) inside the park. On the NPS route line, about 550 ft inside the park line. Stay within 100 ft of the route. The NPS Trail of Tears Northern Route line runs about 0.4 mi through the WMA. Much of the historic route follows today's roads, so confirm you are on WMA land within 100 ft of the route and look for official signage. The Lewis &amp; Clark auto tour route (Lola Rd) also runs along the park edge nearby. Possible only: do your own research. GPS pins are guides only: they get you to the area of a possible 2-fer, not to a confirmed spot. Confirm on the ground that you are inside every park and within 100 ft of the trail or water. Planning tool only: do your own research.</desc><sym>Flag, Green</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="37.36804" lon="-88.43962"><name>2-fer US-4572 + US-3823</name><cmt>Guide pin: Inside the park next to the auto tour route (Lola Rd)</cmt><desc>US-4572 Lewis and Clark National Historic Trail + US-3823 Livingston County Wildlife Management Area. Inside the park next to the auto tour route (Lola Rd). Within about 75 ft of the signed auto tour route in our map data, just inside the park line. Stay inside the park. The signed Lewis &amp; Clark auto tour route (Lola Rd) runs along the WMA line here. The NPS calls the auto tour route part of the trail, so a spot inside the WMA within 100 ft of a signed stretch may count. Look for the brown Lewis and Clark Trail / AUTO TOUR ROUTE sign. Not on the river. Possible only: do your own research. (The Trail of Tears Northern Route also crosses this WMA about 1,000 ft to the west; no single spot is within 100 ft of both.) GPS pins are guides only: they get you to the area of a possible 2-fer, not to a confirmed spot. Confirm on the ground that you are inside every park and within 100 ft of the trail or water. Planning tool only: do your own research.</desc><sym>Flag, Green</sym></wpt>
